# Frontend configuration — the public site and the dashboard.
#
# Copy to `.env.local`. Every VITE_* value is **public**: it is compiled into
# the bundle a visitor downloads, so an origin belongs here and a key never
# does.

# ---- CMS API -------------------------------------------------------
# Origin of the API in `server/`.
#
# Leave it EMPTY and the site does not call the API at all: it renders the
# content snapshot embedded in the bundle and makes no request. That is the
# correct setting for a static preview deployment, and it is why adding the
# CMS changed nothing about a build without one.
#
# Set it and the site fetches `/api/v1/content/published` after first paint
# and swaps in anything newer. The first paint is unchanged either way — the
# embedded snapshot renders immediately, so there is no spinner and no shift.
#
# The dashboard at `/admin.html` needs this set to reach the API at all.
# Without it the dashboard posts to the Vite dev server, which answers 404 for
# anything under /api — and the error reads as a broken login rather than as
# missing configuration.
#
# Must match `PORT` in `server/.env`. 3100 rather than 3000 because 3000 is the
# default for Next.js and most other frameworks; two servers on one port is
# worse than it sounds, because they can bind different address families and
# the OS reports no conflict at all.
VITE_CMS_API="http://localhost:3100"

# ---- Preview gate --------------------------------------------------
# A digit code shown before the site loads. Leave it unset — as here — and
# there is no gate, which is what a public deployment wants.
#
# It is a display barrier, not security: the code is compiled into the bundle
# and is findable in seconds with the developer tools. Use it to keep casual
# visitors out of an unfinished staging preview, never to protect anything.
# Real protection is HTTP Basic Auth at the web server.
#
# VITE_ACCESS_CODE="753159"

# ---- Application form ----------------------------------------------
# Where the site's Bewerbung dialog POSTs its multipart form.
#
# Unset, the dialog shows the upload option as unavailable and the e-mail path
# still works end to end — it never pretends to accept a dossier it would
# drop. The API in `server/` implements exactly the fields the form already
# sends, so this is all the wiring it needs:
VITE_BEWERBUNG_ENDPOINT="http://localhost:3100/api/v1/applications"
